uhCkkxbZWKB0096XcqG_rDGk2xUkXTrjQAhmpwhJ.description:
{
  "outcome": "An LLM works with the live clarity tree through a direct, in-app API — reading current state and proposing typed edits — instead of the file-based diff round-trip (branch I export/import). Because it reads and proposes against the live tree, the tolerant-merge import (branch I, \"tolerant to changes since last export\") becomes redundant: there is no stale export to reconcile.",
  "handle": "llm-api",
  "spec": "A flow view of the live loop belongs here (a diagram, not a list): the LLM reads the tree for context, proposes typed edits, the human reviews and commits them, and the LLM executes the work a ready leaf specifies. The detailed specs live on the leaves below.",
  "principle": "The human is the only committer. The LLM reads and proposes; it never writes to the DHT directly.",
  "artifacts": [
    {
      "type": "reference",
      "label": "flow diagram: the live LLM↔tree loop (placeholder)",
      "uri": ""
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkajTxTaDzgVavVrnuyJdES3qpbYKmsb1CjgA.description:
{
  "outcome": "The LLM reads the live tree through a read tool (read_tree): nodes, typed fields, connections, and authored achievement status. This is the read half of the MCP-style tool surface; the propose/write half lives in the conversational-editing branch's draft pipeline, so reads are a general capability while writes always go through review. The read shape reuses the agent-consumable export (branch D). Computed achievement/readiness is surfaced here once branch B's computed layer exists — read_tree exposes whatever the snapshot holds; today that is authored scope/achievementStatus.",
  "spec": "read_tree() -> the live project snapshot (outcomes with their typed fields, connections, tags, authored scope/achievementStatus), typed so the harness targets it without scraping the UI. (read_tree and propose_edits together form the MCP-style surface; propose_edits is specified under the draft pipeline.) Computed achievement/readiness appears here once branch B produces it — a relies-on, not built in this leaf.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "The harness calls read_tree and receives the live project's nodes, typed fields, connections and authored status",
      "evaluator": "executable",
      "evidence": "2026-06-17: harness called mcp__acorn__read_tree and received the live ProjectSnapshot — 70 outcomes, 69 connections, 4 tags; top-level keys {projectMeta, outcomes, connections, outcomeMembers, outcomeComments, entryPoints, tags}. Each outcome carries its typed fields in the description (parseable OutcomeFields) plus scope.achievementStatus. Result was 137,556 chars — too large for the harness context window in one piece, but fully received and parsed."
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkqVoV_Lq_Pr_l6nJVd9OjGlephjIKCRLsR_u.description:
{
  "outcome": "Distinct from improving the clarity OF the tree, this is doing the work the tree clarifies: when a human manually triggers a ready leaf, the LLM reads that leaf's outcome, spec and completion criteria, performs the work (e.g. writes the code), then posts results — completion verdicts and produced artifacts — back onto the node (relies on branches D and E). Manual trigger is the prototype step before readiness assessment (branch G) gates it automatically. A clear, unambiguous leaf is exactly what makes this easy to direct.",
  "handle": "do-it-trigger",
  "spec": "A 'Do this leaf' action on a sufficiently-clear leaf hands its clarity fields to the harness as the work spec; the harness executes and returns artifacts + verdicts, which attach to the node (relies on branches D2/D3, E). No automatic triggering yet — the human picks the leaf.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "A human triggers a ready leaf; the LLM performs the work and posts at least one artifact and a completion verdict back to that node",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkhaJ3vdKL7IwIPfysP2MWwyBWf7PoxBhfsnX.description:
{
