uhCkkollIHEqoy4vLLIBv7leqZQEduWqhqSOSsd6.description:
{
  "outcome": "Proposed tree edits are reviewed in a non-committed draft before they touch the DHT — the tree-editing analogue of an agent's PLAN MODE (propose → review/edit → approve → execute). A draft is an uncommitted overlay on the live tree; nothing persists until the human confirms.",
  "spec": "A flow view of the draft lifecycle belongs here: a proposed edit set opens the draft, it renders in glow-mode, the human edits or rejects, and confirm commits (or discard drops). It reuses branch I's glow + diff executor; where the ACP harness has a native plan mode, surface its proposals as the draft. The detailed specs live on the leaves below.",
  "principle": "Proposed edits are inert until a human confirms them; the draft never persists on its own (the agent plan-mode contract).",
  "artifacts": [
    {
      "type": "reference",
      "label": "agent plan mode (propose → approve → execute); flow diagram of the draft lifecycle (placeholder)",
      "uri": ""
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkrsQwLSyxvq1zZ4hPfuxFydn4Qpo1P9kZ-NV.description:
{
  "outcome": "The propose half of the MCP-style tool surface: the LLM submits a typed edit set (add / update / remove of outcomes, fields, connections) via propose_edits, which opens a draft rather than writing to the DHT. The edit shape reuses branch I's ProjectDiff. This is the single entry point by which any proposed change becomes a reviewable draft.",
  "spec": "propose_edits(diff: ProjectDiff) -> opens or updates the ephemeral draft, never a direct write. The edit shape is branch I's ProjectDiff. Called by the harness over ACP.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "propose_edits opens a draft and writes nothing to the DHT",
      "evaluator": "executable",
      "evidence": "2026-06-17: the harness called mcp__acorn__propose_edits with a ProjectDiff (outcomes.updated) multiple times this session; each returned {opened:true, note: 'Draft opened for human review — inert until Confirm. No DHT write.'}. The live tree only changed after the human clicked Confirm (read_tree node committed with the human's editorAgentPubKey), confirming propose itself writes nothing to the DHT. The diff shape accepted is branch I's ProjectDiff (per-collection added/updated/removed keyed by actionHash)."
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkvHBj2NSn7euPfohXSdHTWKrdSXPDDgFiXAd.description:
{
  "outcome": "The open draft renders using the existing glow-mode — changed nodes glow amber, each carries its +/~/- badge, and the view fits to the changed set (branch I i3a/i3b/i3c) — but driven by the UNCOMMITTED draft diff layered over the live tree. Added nodes show as draft (ghost) nodes, updated nodes show their proposed values, removed nodes are marked. Nothing is written to cells; this is a live preview.",
  "spec": "Render the tree with the draft diff overlaid: merge the draft's added/updated outcomes into the render set (flagged as draft), mark removed ones, and reuse setChangedOutcomes + perOutcomeChangeStats so glow and badges show the proposed deltas. The draft diff lives in ephemeral state, not in the persisted project.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "An open draft lights the affected nodes in glow-mode with correct +/~/- badges and fits them in view, with nothing written to the DHT",
      "evaluator": "human",
      "evidence": "2026-06-17: human (Eric) confirmed live that an open draft renders affected nodes in amber glow-mode with +/~/- badges and fits them in view, and that nothing is written to the DHT until Confirm — verified across several drafts this session. (Replaces the earlier accidental placeholder evidence.)"
    }
  ]
}

uhCkks1U_MBtCl_8crFFYaDCKaYT2niJK6Mktvnu.description:
{
  "outcome": "While a draft is open, the human refines it before committing: edit a proposed node's content or fields, and accept or reject individual proposed changes. Edits mutate the in-memory draft diff — not the DHT — so the human shapes the final edit set. Rejecting a change drops it from the draft; editing a proposed value updates it in place.",
  "spec": "Per-change controls: accept/reject per node and inline editing that writes back into the draft ProjectDiff (added/updated entries). The draft diff is recomputed and re-rendered as the human edits, keeping glow + badges in sync. No cell writes until confirm.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "A human edits a proposed node and rejects another within the draft; the committed result reflects exactly the accepted + edited set",
      "evaluator": "human",
      "evidence": "Works (-eric).  Note that: the eject half already proven: in the prior draft an L4C-REJECT-MARKER change to this node was per-node rejected and never committed, while a sibling node (L4b) committed. "
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkDfTU-c2QlHpNSUxnzeB1GjJx_ZXyqOxystw.description:
{
  "outcome": "A single 'Confirm' commits the draft's accepted edits to the DHT in one step — reusing the branch I diff executor (applyProjectDiffToCell) — and clears the draft. 'Discard' drops the draft with no trace. Until one of these, nothing the LLM proposed has persisted. After commit, the just-committed nodes stay glowing (branch I) so the human sees what landed.",
  "spec": "Confirm: run applyProjectDiffToCell over the accepted subset of the draft diff, then clear ephemeral draft state and light the committed nodes via the normal post-apply path. Discard: clear the draft state only. Both exit draft mode.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "Confirming a draft persists exactly the accepted edits and they survive reload; discarding leaves the tree unchanged",
      "evaluator": "executable",
      "evidence": "2026-06-17: Confirm half — propose_edits drafts on the read_tree node and on this branch's propose node were Confirmed and persisted to the DHT (read back across turns with the human's editorAgentPubKey, surviving reload). Discard half — a throwaway draft put a 'DISCARD-TEST MARKER' in this node's title, a checked throwaway task, and a marker evidence string; after the human clicked Discard, a fresh read_tree showed zero occurrences of the marker anywhere in the tree and this node byte-for-byte unchanged (NotAchieved, its original 4 tasks). Discard wrote nothing."
    }
  ]
}

uhCkkmoSfYhq3OvHJd_BtfbGaMkgg7tyP7H38Qab.description:
{
  "outcome": "The skill states the vocabulary an LLM must use correctly: a node is a positive target-state outcome carrying typed fields; scope is Uncertain (a branch still decomposing) or Small (a buildable leaf); achievement is computed, not toggled; and dependencies are typed (constituted-from vs relies-on). Fluency in the ontology is what lets the LLM emit well-formed nodes."
}

uhCkkXRStBlSWDAjYKbFaCp7i3gDmEAIyafhPFt2.description:
{
  "outcome": "The skill encodes the rules that make a tree's structure meaningful: a composed-of dependency is an explicit parent→child edge, never prose; each node is self-contained; a parent never restates or counts its children; relies-on is a typed edge, not hierarchy. When these hold, prioritization and achievement roll up correctly."
}

uhCkkdXxDjnVGWdqoyz9uxadkg25d-R3G5dukJDQ.description:
{
  "outcome": "The skill guides breaking an unclear or too-large outcome into children, where each child is something the parent needs in order to be possible and the children together are sufficient for it. It ties the decision to readiness signals (branch G): low readiness or an open unknown is the cue to decompose."
}

uhCkkf4EITalAfK6ncejMm8cLKBhnMW1na8ERfaT.description:
{
  "outcome": "The skill places detail correctly: most specs live low, on the Small leaves that will be built; a branch's spec is not a restatement of its children but a higher-level view — often a flow or interaction diagram — of how the parts below it fit together. Completion criteria attach wherever they can be evaluated."
}

uhCkk6fTYjIkyHxT2JEpuDbCm7OC5t7LuNNkVzEH.description:
{
  "outcome": "The skill describes how the LLM works a tree in practice: read the live tree for context, propose typed edits that open a draft for the human to review and commit, and never write to the DHT directly. It is the LLM-facing companion to the human-approved draft flow."
}

uhCkkvM3LSR0xLrgOh2690itwqmRob4BHfOAA_xV.description:
{
  "outcome": "Humans and LLMs refer to any node unambiguously — in chat, in proposals, across sessions — through a referent that survives the tree being rearranged. The foundation is the content-addressed actionHash (stable by construction; the 6-digit hashCodeId is its stable short rendering), with memorable, human-friendly layers resolving down to it. Positional labels on their own ('i3c', 'branch L') are ephemeral by nature; identity that survives rearrangement is content-addressed, never positional.",
  "spec": "A reference resolves through one canonical identity (the content-addressed actionHash) with memorable layers above it; the detailed specs live on the leaves below.",
  "principle": "Stable identity is content-addressed and never positional; every memorable or casual layer resolves down to it and is never itself stored as identity."
}

uhCkkCMovpTArrSO4ZyFQP7qE2YfWqr5MnVhBJZk.description:
{
  "outcome": "A runnable example demonstrates that Acorn's LLM harness is backend-agnostic: the same in-app chat loop — read the live tree, propose typed edits, a human reviews and Confirms a draft — works against LLM backends other than Claude, and in particular against a model running fully on the local machine with no network egress. The transport is already model-agnostic; this branch proves it in practice across more than one concrete backend and records how to reproduce each.",
  "spec": "Relies-on the achieved ACP-transport node 'Acorn connects to a local LLM harness over ACP' (actionHash uhCkkRcJuqggo9veFW-mFGr7La_kE20NPMcBucdvUOyiJxkcAfNxx): that node owns the conversation + transport; this branch owns proving the end-to-end loop against concrete backends. The mechanism is already in place — sidecar.js spawns whatever ACORN_HARNESS_CMD is and speaks pure ACP, so a backend is selected by a dev script that sets that env (and, for Anthropic-API-shaped agents, ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL). The children carry the detail: prove a non-Claude hosted backend (largely done — harness:gemini, harness:openrouter) then prove a fully-local model. The binding constraint everywhere is tool-calling fidelity — the backend must actually call read_tree and emit a well-formed propose_edits ProjectDiff — not transport.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
      "statement": "The identical in-app chat loop — read_tree → propose_edits → human Confirm → committed draft — succeeds regardless of which backend dev script launched the harness (a hosted non-Claude backend AND a fully-local model), proving the loop is backend-agnostic",
      "evaluator": "human"
    }
  ],
  "handle": "harness-backends"
}

uhCkkW9UKx4A5MPeUKZcz9UaEKmPX9A9N_OXzI3K.description:
{
  "outcome": "Any node can be given an optional short handle — a human-chosen slug like 'read-tree' or 'draft-glow' — stored as an authored field and unique within the project. Because it is author-assigned rather than positional it survives rearrangement; because it is words it is memorable. A node with no handle simply has none.",
  "handle": "node-handle",
  "spec": "Add an optional `handle` slug to the node's OutcomeFields ([a-z0-9-]). Enforce uniqueness within a project on assignment; on collision reject or suggest a variant. Empty/unset is valid. Round-trips through parseFields/serializeFields and is editable like any other field.",
  "completionCriteria": [
    {
